  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Given that $f(x)=(x-2{)}^{2},$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (2,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-1,5)$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,2]$",
  "worked_solution": "The graph of the function $f(x)=(x-2{)}^{2},$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units  to  the right  $f(x)=(x-2{)}^{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/1e3ac550e35a3dc93b3e26e5834f763847b04f5133de02e368124f54dd2b9dd6.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-1,5).$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/3ebb316ca122b6786b8ef501fc7c56f8e0e68c8cfc32e5801c493d38382fc26c.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (2,\\infty )$  and $x\\in (-\\infty ,2].$  On these intervals, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/86c7c9cacc7a3868565f33130563ffed0ca423aa087e76cc08d103ea171822ed.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
